{"cuisine/iranian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/iranian-cuisine","n":"Iranian cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Iran","intro":[["Iranian cuisine comprises the culinary traditions of Iran. Due to the historically common usage of the term \"Persia\" to refer to Iran in the Western world, it is alternatively known as Persian cuisine, despite Persians being only one of a multitude of Iranian ethnic groups who have contributed to Iran's culinary traditions."],["Iran has a rich variety of traditional dishes, and has influenced many other cuisines over the ages, among them Caucasian cuisine, Central Asian cuisine, Greek cuisine, Levantine cuisine, Mesopotamian cuisine, Russian cuisine and Turkish cuisine. Aspects of Iranian cuisine have also been significantly adopted by Indian cuisine and Pakistani cuisine through various historical Persianate sultanates that flourished during Muslim rule on the Indian subcontinent, most significantly the Mughal Empire."],["Typical Iranian main dishes are combinations of rice with meat, vegetables and nuts. Herbs are frequently used, such as parsley, fenugreek, chives, mint, savory and coriander, in their fresh and dried forms. Another consistent feature of Persian cuisine is the abundant use of fruits, in combination with various meats as well as in rice dishes; the most commonly used fruits include plums, pomegranates, quince, prunes, apricots, barberries, and raisins. Characteristic Iranian spices and flavourings such as saffron, cardamom, and dried lime and other sources of sour flavoring, cinnamon, turmeric and parsley are mixed and used in various dishes."],["Outside of Iran, Iranian cuisine can be found in cities with significant Iranian diaspora populations, namely London, the San Francisco Bay Area, the Washington metropolitan area, Vancouver, Toronto, Houston and especially Los Angeles and its environs."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"iranian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iranian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Unicorns in the Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"unicornsinthekitchen.com","note":"Iranian-born Shadi HasanzadeNemati's blog with a large Persian recipe section.","url":"https://www.unicornsinthekitchen.com/persian-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Food of Life: Ancient Persian and Modern Iranian Cooking and Ceremonies","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Najmieh Batmanglij, 1986","note":"Standard reference on Iranian cooking from Mage Publishers; ISBN is the 2024 40th anniversary edition.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781949445800","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781949445800"},{"name":"The Saffron Tales: Recipes from the Persian Kitchen","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Yasmin Khan, 2016","note":"Recipes collected while traveling across Iran, published by Bloomsbury.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781408868737","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781408868737"}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":18,"links":[["Akuri","dish/akuri"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Chelow kabab","dish/chelow-kabab"],["Doogh","dish/doogh"],["Falooda","dish/falooda"],["Jujeh kabab","dish/jujeh-kabab"],["Kabab torsh","dish/kabab-torsh"],["Keşkek","dish/keskek"],["Khash (dish)","dish/khash-dish"],["Khoresh karafs","dish/khoresh-karafs"],["Lighvan cheese","dish/lighvan-cheese"],["Nisholda","dish/nisholda"],["Pilaf","dish/pilaf"],["Scorched rice","dish/scorched-rice"],["Sheermal","dish/sheermal"],["Shirazi salad","dish/shirazi-salad"],["Shirin polo","dish/shirin-polo"],["Sholezard","dish/sholezard"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Saffron","ingredient/saffron"],["Mentha","ingredient/mentha"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Rose water","ingredient/rose-water"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Cardamom","ingredient/cardamom"],["Scrambled egg","ingredient/scrambled-egg"],["Scrambled eggs","ingredient/scrambled-eggs"],["Cabbage","ingredient/cabbage"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"],["Kebab","ingredient/kebab"],["Pilaf","ingredient/pilaf"],["Buttermilk","ingredient/buttermilk"],["Yogurt","ingredient/yogurt"],["Ocimum","ingredient/ocimum"],["Rose syrup","ingredient/rose-syrup"],["Sweet basil","ingredient/sweet-basil"],["Vermicelli","ingredient/vermicelli"],["Barley","ingredient/barley"],["Wheat","ingredient/wheat"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Within Iranian cooking","count":3,"links":[["Balochi cuisine","cuisine/balochi-cuisine"],["Caspian cuisine","cuisine/caspian-cuisine"],["Kurdish cuisine","cuisine/kurdish-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/9/9f/Persian_Cuisine.jpg/500px-Persian_Cuisine.jpg","w":500,"h":667,"by":"Asadi","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Persian_Cuisine.jpg"}},"cuisine/karnataka-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/karnataka-cuisine","n":"Karnataka cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["South Indian cuisine","cuisine/south-indian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in India","intro":[["Karnataka cuisine is the cuisine of the Indian state of Karnataka. It is commonly served on a banana leaf, especially for special occasions."],["Its varieties include Mysore and Bangalore cuisine, North Karnataka cuisine, Udupi cuisine, Kodagu and Coorg cuisine, Karavali and coastal cuisine, and Saraswat cuisine. It includes vegan, vegetarian, and meat items, as well as savory and sweet dishes."],["Dishes that originated in Karnataka but have become popular outside the state include idli, rava idli, and Mysore masala dosa. Other Karnataka cuisine items include:"],["Avalakki - Flattened parboiled rice cooked with spices. In Karnataka avalakki can be eaten with majjige or gojju."],["Chitranna - Chitranna is prepared using rice, onion, green chili, lemon or raw mango peel, coriander leaves and turmeric."],["Bisi bele bath- A spicy rice-based dish. It originated in Mysore, Karnataka and from there spread across South India."],["Jolada rotti - The staple diet of most of North Karnataka. It is eaten with pulse curries, ennegayi or assorted chutneys."],["Mysore pak - Sweet dish prepared by using gram flour and ghee"],["Holige (obbattu) - Extremely common sweet dish in Karnataka"],["Neer dosa - Very common in Mangalore and Malenadu regions."],["Ragi mudde - Very common in rural south Karnataka. Eaten with bassaaru and upasaaru."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"karnataka-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karnataka_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Dassana's Veg Recipes (Punjabi)","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"vegrecipesofindia.com","note":"Karnataka section of Dassana Amit's Indian vegetarian recipe blog.","url":"https://www.vegrecipesofindia.com/recipes/karnataka-cuisine/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Chitra's Food Book","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"chitrasfoodbook.com","note":"Karnataka recipes on a South Indian home cook's vegetarian blog.","url":"https://www.chitrasfoodbook.com/recipes/karnataka-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":4,"links":[["Chitranna","dish/chitranna"],["Majjige huli","dish/majjige-huli"],["Pundi","dish/pundi"],["Vangibath","dish/vangibath"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Curd","ingredient/curd"],["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["South Indian cuisine","cuisine/south-indian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Within Karnataka cooking","count":4,"links":[["Dakshina Karnataka cuisine","cuisine/south-karnataka"],["Kodava cuisine","cuisine/kodava-people"],["Udupi cuisine","cuisine/udupi-cuisine"],["Uttara Karnataka cuisine","cuisine/north-karnataka"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/4a/Mango_festival_meal%2C_Bangalore%2C_%282025%29.jpg/500px-Mango_festival_meal%2C_Bangalore%2C_%282025%29.jpg","w":500,"h":322,"by":"Gpkp","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mango_festival_meal%2C_Bangalore%2C_(2025).jpg"}},"ingredient/bryndza":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/bryndza","n":"Bryndza","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 3 dishes across 4 cuisines","intro":[["Bryndza or brynza is a sheep milk cheese made across the countries in Central and Eastern Europe, most notably in Slovakia and Poland. Bryndza cheese is creamy white in appearance, known for its characteristic strong smell and taste. The cheese is white, tangy, crumbly and slightly moist. It has characteristic odor and flavor with a notable taste of butyric acid. The overall flavor sensation begins slightly mild, then goes strong and finally fades to a salty finish. Recipes differ slightly across countries."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-bryndza"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bryndza"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":4,"groups":[["Slovak cuisine","cuisine/slovak-cuisine",[["Bryndzové halušky","dish/bryndzove-halusky"],["Liptauer","dish/liptauer"]]],["Austrian cuisine","cuisine/austrian-cuisine",[["Liptauer","dish/liptauer"]]],["Hungarian cuisine","cuisine/hungarian-cuisine",[["Liptauer","dish/liptauer"]]],["Ukrainian cuisine","cuisine/ukrainian-cuisine",[["Banosh","dish/banosh"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Cornmeal","ingredient/cornmeal"],["Cream","ingredient/cream"],["Maize flour","ingredient/maize-flour"],["Smetana","ingredient/smetana"],["Smetana (dairy product)","ingredient/smetana-dairy-product"],["Corned beef","ingredient/corned-beef"],["Halušky","ingredient/halusky"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Cottage cheese","ingredient/cottage-cheese"],["Goat cheese","ingredient/goat-cheese"],["Quark","ingredient/quark"],["Quark cheese","ingredient/quark-cheese"],["Sheep milk","ingredient/sheep-milk"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/23/Chleb_z_bryndza.jpg/500px-Chleb_z_bryndza.jpg","w":500,"h":265,"by":"Unknown author","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chleb_z_bryndza.jpg"}},"dish/bayonne-ham":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/bayonne-ham","n":"Bayonne ham","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Bayonne ham or jambon de Bayonne  is a cured ham that takes its name from the ancient port city of Bayonne in the far southwest of France, a city located in both the cultural regions of Basque Country and Gascony. It has PGI status."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-bayonne-ham"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bayonne_ham"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine"],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Pardon Your French","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"pardonyourfrench.com","note":"Audrey Le Goff, a French cook, shares classic and regional French home recipes.","url":"https://pardonyourfrench.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"La Cuisine de Géraldine","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"lacuisinedegeraldine.fr","note":"French home cooking blog by Géraldine Leverd, also author of The New French Kitchen.","url":"https://lacuisinedegeraldine.fr/en/about-us","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"New Caledonia Tourism: Kanak cooking","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"newcaledonia.travel","note":"Official tourism board overview of Kanak food, including bougna cooked in an earth oven.","url":"https://www.newcaledonia.travel/nz/gas