,"sub":"Dish from Korean cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Jokbal is a Korean dish consisting of pig's trotters cooked with soy sauce and spices. It is usually braised in a combination of soy sauce, ginger, garlic, and rice wine. Additional ingredients used can include onion, leeks, garlic, cinnamon, and black pepper."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-jokbal"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jokbal"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Ham hock","ingredient/ham-hock"],["Pig's trotter","ingredient/pig-s-trotter"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Maangchi","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"maangchi.com","note":"Maangchi's Korean recipe site and YouTube channel, one of the best known English sources for Korean cooking.","url":"https://www.maangchi.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"},{"name":"Korean Bapsang","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"koreanbapsang.com","note":"Home-style Korean recipes from Hyosun, a Korean-American mom.","url":"https://www.koreanbapsang.com/category/recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Korean cuisine","links":[["Bap (rice dish)","dish/bap-rice-dish"],["Bibim-guksu","dish/bibim-guksu"],["Bibimbap","dish/bibimbap"],["Bosintang","dish/bosintang"],["Cheonggukjang","dish/cheonggukjang"],["Doenjang","dish/doenjang"],["Garak-guksu","dish/garak-guksu"],["Gochujang","dish/gochujang"],["Gopchang","dish/gopchang"],["Hwajeon","dish/hwajeon"],["Jijimi","dish/jijimi"],["Korean restaurant","dish/korean-restaurant"],["Maekjeok","dish/maekjeok"],["Maeun-tang","dish/maeun-tang"],["Makchang","dish/makchang"],["Mandu-guk","dish/mandu-guk"],["Mandu-gwa","dish/mandu-gwa"],["Meat jun","dish/meat-jun"],["Meju","dish/meju"],["Morkovcha","dish/morkovcha"],["Scorched rice","dish/scorched-rice"],["Seon (food)","dish/seon-food"],["Sungnyung","dish/sungnyung"],["Tteokbokki","dish/tteokbokki"],["Wakame","dish/wakame"],["Yakgwa","dish/yakgwa"],["Yukpo","dish/yukpo"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/44/Jokbal-namdaemunmarket.jpg/500px-Jokbal-namdaemunmarket.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Kbarends","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jokbal-namdaemunmarket.jpg"}},"ingredient/schnitzel":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/schnitzel","n":"Schnitzel","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Schnitzel is a thin slice of meat. The meat is usually thinned by pounding with a meat tenderizer. Most commonly, the meat is breaded before frying. Breaded schnitzel is popular in many countries and is made using veal, pork, chicken, mutton, beef, or turkey. Schnitzel originated as Wiener schnitzel and is very similar to other breaded meat dishes."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-schnitzel"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schnitzel"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Serbian cuisine","cuisine/serbian-cuisine",[["Karađorđeva šnicla","dish/kara-or-eva-snicla"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Bread crumbs","ingredient/bread-crumbs"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Kaymak","ingredient/kaymak"],["Steak","ingredient/steak"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/22/Breitenlesau_Krug_Br%C3%A4u_Schnitzel.JPG/500px-Breitenlesau_Krug_Br%C3%A4u_Schnitzel.JPG","w":500,"h":333,"by":"User: Benreis at wikivoyage shared","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Breitenlesau_Krug_Br%C3%A4u_Schnitzel.JPG"}},"ingredient/corn":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/corn","n":"Corn","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Maize, also known as corn, is a tall stout grass that produces cereal grain. The leafy stalk of the plant gives rise to male inflorescences or tassels which produce pollen, and female inflorescences called ears. The ears yield grain, known as kernels or seeds. In modern commercial varieties, these are usually yellow or white; other varieties can be of many colors. Maize was domesticated by indigenous peoples in southern Mexico about 9,000 years ago from wild teosinte. Native Americans planted it alongside beans and squashes in the Three Sisters polyculture."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-corn"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corn"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Brazilian cuisine","cuisine/brazilian-cuisine",[["Manuê","dish/manue"]]]]},{"h":"Where it's grown","grown":{"what":"Maize (corn)","years":"2020 to 2024","url":"https://www.fao.org/faostat/en/#data/QCL","world":1194842295,"top":[["United States of America","cuisine/american-cuisine",370796660],["China","cuisine/chinese-cuisine",278837460],["Brazil","cuisine/brazilian-cuisine",109781604],["Argentina","cuisine/argentine-cuisine",55372549],["India","cuisine/indian-cuisine",35054811],["Ukraine","cuisine/ukrainian-cuisine",31296260],["Mexico","cuisine/mexican-cuisine",26671534],["South Africa","cuisine/south-african-cuisine",15381709],["Russia","cuisine/russian-cuisine",14786645],["Canada","cuisine/canadian-cuisine",14695763],["Indonesia","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",14556896],["France","cuisine/french-cuisine",13493422],["Nigeria","cuisine/nigerian-cuisine",12062734],["Ethiopia","cuisine/ethiopian-cuisine",11550100],["Pakistan","cuisine/pakistani-cuisine",9677670]]}},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Sugar cane molasses","ingredient/sugar-cane-molasses"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/e/e3/Zea_mays_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-283.jpg","w":500,"h":681,"by":"Franz Eugen Köhler, Köhler's Medizinal-Pflanzen","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zea_mays_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-283.jpg"}},"ingredient/black-tea":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/black-tea","n":"Black tea","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Black tea (also literally translated as red tea from various East Asian languages) is a type of tea that is more oxidized than oolong, yellow, white, and green teas. Black tea is generally stronger in flavour than other teas. All five types are made from leaves of the shrub (or small tree) Camellia sinensis, though Camellia taliensis is also rarely used."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-black-tea"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_tea"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine",[["Masala chai","dish/masala-chai"]]]]},{"h":"Where it's grown","grown":{"what":"Tea leaves","years":"2020 to 2024","url":"https://www.fao.org/faostat/en/#data/QCL","world":30884451,"top":[["China","cuisine/chinese-cuisine",14717540],["India","cuisine/indian-cuisine",5999112],["Kenya","cuisine/kenyan-cuisine",2632820],["Sri Lanka","cuisine/sri-lankan-cuisine",1541934],["Turkey","cuisine/turkish-cuisine",1388924],["Vietnam","cuisine/vietnamese-cuisine",1110478],["Indonesia","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",643600],["Bangladesh","cuisine/bangladeshi-cuisine",418725],["Uganda","cuisine/ugandan-cuisine",408276],["Argentina","cuisine/argentine-cuisine",345491],["Japan","cuisine/japanese-cuisine",326280],["Malawi","cuisine/malawian-cuisine",251941],["Rwanda","cuisine/rwandan-cuisine",153829],["Tanzania","cuisine/tanzanian-cuisine",131757],["Nepal","cuisine/nepalese-cuisine",126095]]}},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/8/8a/Cup_of_black_tea.JPG/500px-Cup_of_black_tea.JPG","w":500,"h":466,"by":"AntanO","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cup_of_black_tea.JPG"}},"ingredient/orange":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/orange","n":"Orange","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 2 cuisines","intro":[["The orange, also called sweet orange to distinguish it from the bitter orange (Citrus × aurantium), is the fruit of a tree in the family Rutaceae. Botanically, this is the hybrid Citrus × sinensis, between the pomelo (Citrus maxima) and the mandarin orange (Citrus reticulata). The chloroplast genome, and therefore the maternal line, is that of pomelo. Hybrids of the sweet orange form later types of mandarin and the grapefruit. The sweet orange has had its full genome sequenced."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-orange"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orange_(fruit)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Meuseukat","dish/meuseukat"],["Natinombur","dish/natinombur"]]],["Acehnese cuisine","cuisine/acehnese-cuisine",[["Meuseukat","dish/meuseukat"]]]]},{"h":"Where it's grown","grown":{"what":"Oranges","years":"2020 to 2024","url":"https://www.fao.org/faostat/en/#data/QCL","world":68304957,"top":[["Brazil","cuisine/brazilian-cuisine",16641588],["China","cuisine/chinese-cuisine",7580000],["Mexico","cuisine/mexican-cuisine",4775367],["India","cuisine/indian-cuisine",3940375],["Egypt","cuisine/egyptian-cuisine",3594229],["United States of America","cuisine/american-cuisine",3333000],["Spain","cuisine/spanish-cuisine",3077212],["Indonesia","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",2684054],["Iran","cuisine/iranian-cuisine",2257811],["Italy","cuisine/italian-cuisine",1794016],["Turkey","cuisine/turkish-cuisine",1663862],["Vietnam","cuisine/vietnamese-cuisine",1653410],["South Africa","cuisine/south-african-cuisine",1627635],["Algeria","cuisine/algerian-cuisine",1220985],["Argentina","cuisine/argentine-cuisine",1033793]]}},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Pineapple","ingredient/pineapple"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Andaliman","ingredient/andaliman"],["Bird's eye chili","ingredient/bird-s-eye-chili"],["Candlenut","ingredient/candlenut"],["Etlingera elatior","ingredient/etlingera-elatior"],["Galangal","ingredient/galangal"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Nile tilapia","ingredient/nile-tilapia"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/e/e3/Oranges_-_whole-halved-segment.jpg/500px-Oranges_-_whole-halved-segment.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Ivar Leidus","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oranges_-_whole-halved-segment.jpg"}},"ingredient/clitoria-ternatea":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/clitoria-ternatea","n":"Clitoria ternatea","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Clitoria ternatea, commonly known as Shankhupushpam (conch-shaped flower), Asian pigeonwings, bluebellvine, blue pea, butterfly pea, cordofan pea, or Darwin pea, is a plant in the family Fabaceae. It is named after the Indonesian island of Ternate. In Indian Ayurveda it is commonly known by the name aparajita."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-clitoria-ternatea"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clitoria_ternatea"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Malay cuisine","cuisine/malay-cuisine",[["Nasi kerabu","dish/nasi-kerabu"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Budu (sauce)","ingredient/budu-sauce"],["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"],["Fish","ingredient/fish"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Sambal","ingredient/sambal"],["Turmeric","ingredient/turmeric"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/2a/Asian_pigeonwings_in_Bhopal_04.jpg/500px-Asian_pigeonwings_in_Bhopal_04.jpg","w":500,"h":667,"by":"Suyash Dwivedi","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Asian_pigeonwings_in_Bhopal_04.jpg"}},"dish/plecing-kangkung":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/plecing-kangkung","n":"Plecing kangkung","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Plecing kangkung is an Indonesian spicy water spinach dish from the island of Bali and Lombok. Plecing kangkung is made from blanched water spinach leaves (Ipomoea aquatica) and served cold with plecing sambal made from ground red chili pepper, shallot, garlic, bird's eye chili, candlenut, kaffir lime, shrimp paste, salt, and sugar. As a side for the Lomboknese dish ayam taliwang and Balinese dish ayam betutu, plecing kangkung is also usually served with additional ingredients such as bean sprouts, string beans, fried peanuts, and urap's grated spicy coconut dressing."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-plecing-kangkung"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plecing_kangkung"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Balinese cuisine","cuisine/balinese-cuisine"],["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":6,"links":[["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Ipomoea aquatica","ingredient/ipomoea-aquatica"],["Kangkung","ingredient/kangkung"],["Mung bean sprout","ingredient/mung-bean-sprout"],["Sambal","ingredient/sambal"],["Shrimp paste","ingredient/shrimp-paste"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Daily Cooking Quest","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"dailycookingquest.com","note":"Indonesian home cook Anita's blog with a large archive of Indonesian recipes.","url":"https://dailycookingquest.com/cuisines/indonesian.html","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Balinese cuisine","links":[["Sate lilit","dish/sate-lilit"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/f/fe/Pelecing_kangkung.JPG/500px-Pelecing_kangkung.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Midori","lic":"CC BY 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pelecing_kangkung.JPG"}},"dish/small-sausage-in-large-sausage":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/small-sausage-in-large-sausage","n":"Small sausage in large sausage","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Taiwan cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Small sausage in large sausage is a snack sausage sandwich invented in Taiwan in the late 20th century. A segment of Taiwanese pork sausage is wrapped in a (slightly bigger and fatter) sticky rice patty, and usually served chargrilled. Deluxe versions are available in night markets in Taiwan, with condiments such as pickled bokchoy, pickled cucumber, garlic, hot peppers, wasabi, and thick soy sauce paste to complement the taste."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-small-sausage-in-large-sausage"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Small_sausage_in_large_sausage"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Taiwan cuisine","cuisine/taiwan-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Cooking in Chinglish","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"cookinginchinglish.com","note":"Taiwanese home recipes from Edwina, who grew up in Taiwan.","url":"https://cookinginchinglish.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Tiny Urban Kitchen: Taiwanese Recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"tinyurbankitchen.com","note":"Small set of Taiwanese recipes from Jen Che, a Taiwanese American blogger in Hong Kong.","url":"https://tinyurbankitchen.com/taiwanese-recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Taiwan cuisine","links":[["Ageh (food)","dish/ageh-food"],["Alivongvong","dish/alivongvong"],["Apple bread","dish/appl